<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ezo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yezo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ezo was the Japanese name for the lands north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a>, especially the island now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a>. From the 16th century the Matsumae clan controlled its southern tip and the Ainu trade, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> took direct control at times of Russian pressure. In 1869, after the short-lived Republic of Ezo, it was renamed Hokkaido and opened to settlement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ezo.jpg" alt="Ezo" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alain Manneson Mallet (1603-1706) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mallet-TerredeGesso.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
